; Lifecycle tests of the protocol loop, layer 2 and layer 3 of the test
; design: the test client drives the built server for the replies, and a
; framed pipe drives it where only bytes will do (the exit codes and the
; frame reader's refusals). Both executables are dependencies, not
; libraries, because every test runs the real binary.
;
; Every input of these tests is either a `.t` file, which dune adds to the
; stanza itself, or one of the five entries below: the three helpers the tests
; run from the sandbox and the two executables they drive. A file a test
; reads that is not listed here would not invalidate its recorded result, so
; a fixture added later belongs in this list too.
;
; The binaries stay declared even though dune would build them anyway: the
; dependency is what makes a rebuilt server or client re-run the cram tests
; instead of leaving a recorded pass from the previous binary in place.
;
; `make test` runs these tests on every run: `dune runtest --force` does not
; re-execute a cram test whose record is current (measured, see docs/ci.md
; "The test step"), so `scripts/runtest.sh` first takes the records back with
; `dune clean` and then runs the tests. It fails the step when a `.t` file
; here has no record after a green run. The stanza must never be given
; `(files ...)`, `(applies_to ...)` or an `(enabled_if false)`: each of those
; leaves a `.t` file in the tree with no record, which is exactly what the
; runner refuses.

(cram
 (deps
  frames.sh
  bounded.sh
  record-pid.sh
  %{exe:../../client/jide_test_client.exe}
  %{exe:../../../lsp-server/native/main.exe}))
